.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KUBE-CLUSTER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kube-cluster
.Nd build a highly available Kubernetes cluster of ZFS-cloned virtual machines on this host, and run it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm bootstrap
.Op Fl -cps Ar n
.Op Fl -workers Ar n
.Op Fl -ram Ar mb
.Op Fl -cpus Ar n
.Op Fl -disk Ar gb
.Op Fl -image Ar path
.Nm
.Cm golden | status | stop | mesh-repair | vip-float
.Nm
.Cm start
.Op Ar n
.Nm
.Cm scale
.Ar n
.Nm
.Cm destroy
.Op Fl -all
.Op Fl -yes
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Cm bootstrap
builds a cluster in one command:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
a cloud image of the host's family \(em Debian or Ubuntu, or Fedora for
every RPM host \(em imported to a ZFS volume and booted with cloud-init;
.It
.Xr kube-setup 8
inside it, from the host's darksite when there is one, so the build
needs no internet; then shut down and snapshotted as the golden, kept
for next time;
.It
the control planes and workers cloned from the golden, in well under a
second each, and booted;
.It
the WireGuard mesh among them and the host;
.It
.Xr kube-init 8
on the first control plane, then the others and the workers joined with
.Ql kubeadm join ,
driven from this host with a fresh token for each attempt;
.It
.Xr kube-smoke-test 8
on the control plane.
.El
.Pp
Three control planes is the default, so that losing one loses nothing,
behind an API address that kube-vip moves between them.
The count is cut to an odd number that fits the host's memory; the start
of a run says what was chosen.
With no workers the control planes run the workloads.
.Pp
The cluster's machines do not start when the host does, so that a first
boot does not start them all at once;
.Cm start
does.
It runs itself under
.Xr sudo 8 .
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm bootstrap
As above.
.It Cm golden
Build only the golden.
.It Cm status
The cluster's machines and volumes, and the nodes when the control plane
is running.
.It Cm start Op Ar n
Start the cluster and wait for it to be Ready; with
.Ar n ,
only that many workers.
.It Cm stop
Shut it down, workers first; the machines and volumes stay.
.It Cm scale Ar n
Add
.Ar n
workers, then repair the mesh.
.It Cm mesh-repair , Cm mesh
Make every node a peer of every other and of the host, and drop peers of
nodes that no longer exist; only differences are touched.
.It Cm vip-float
Move a cluster of several control planes from the fixed API address to
one kube-vip moves to whichever control plane leads.
.It Cm destroy
Remove the cluster's machines and volumes, after typing
.Ql destroy ;
the golden is kept unless
.Fl -all
is given.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -cps Ar n , Fl -control-planes Ar n
Control planes; default 3.
.It Fl -workers Ar n
Workers; default 0.
.It Fl -ram Ar mb , Fl -cpus Ar n , Fl -disk Ar gb
Each node's size; default 4096 MB, 2 processors, 40 GB.
.It Fl -image Ar path
The cloud image, instead of finding one.
.It Fl -all
With
.Cm destroy ,
the golden too.
.It Fl -yes , Fl y
With
.Cm destroy ,
no question; for a script, the web console or ssh.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev CLUSTER_NAME
The machines' name prefix; default
.Ql kldload .
.It Ev KUBE_VIP_ADDRESS , Ev METALLB_POOL , Ev METALLB_POOL_NAME , Ev POD_CIDR , Ev SVC_CIDR
Passed to
.Xr kube-init 8 .
.It Ev KUBE_CLUSTER_YES
1 is
.Fl -yes .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /root/darksite/
The host's darksite, lent to the golden build.
.It Pa /root/.kube/config
The cluster's admin configuration, on the host.
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/
One log per node joined.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done as asked.
.It 1
No cluster, or a failure, named.
.It 2
A usage error, or
.Cm destroy
with no terminal to ask on and no
.Fl -yes .
Anything but
.Ql destroy
typed at its question stops it with exit 0.
.It 3
.Cm bootstrap :
the cluster is up with problems \(em a worker, readiness, the smoke test
\(em named at the end.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop with no cluster, on 2026-09-30, with this release's
.Nm
first on the path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kube-cluster status
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Kubernetes Cluster Status
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

VMs:
NAME                 STATE        RAM        VCPUS  ZVOL USED
NAME        USED  AVAIL

ZFS:
NAME                            USED  REFER  VOLSIZE

Kubernetes: control plane not running

══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
Building and using one; not run for this page:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kube-cluster bootstrap --workers 3
# kube-cluster stop
# kube-cluster start 1
# kube-cluster destroy --yes
.Ed
.Pp
The reference shape, three control planes and three workers, took six
and three quarter minutes on a lab host.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kube-bluegreen 8 ,
.Xr kube-init 8 ,
.Xr kube-network 8 ,
.Xr kube-setup 8 ,
.Xr kube-smoke-test 8 ,
.Xr kube-status 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-27 a golden said to be built offline was found fetching
containerd, kubeadm, Helm and every image from the internet; it builds
from the host's darksite now.
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: the host's own command-line tools, when
the darksite had none, came from the network unverified \(em Helm by piping
a script from a branch head into
.Xr bash 1
as root; and
.Cm status
waited three minutes to report that there was no cluster.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
.Cm destroy
also removes host-wide state the cluster used: the host's WireGuard
keys, its kubeconfig, its
.Pa /etc/hosts
entries.
Rebuilding a golden destroys it with
.Ql zfs destroy -R ,
which takes every clone of it, whoever made them.
